      "sourceMarkdown": "# K1 Unit 1 · Week 2 · Day 1\n\n## Day Metadata\n- Level: K1\n- Unit Number: 1\n- Unit Theme: Friends and Family\n- Week: 2\n- Day: 1\n- Day Type: Power Up\n- Power Up Lesson: PU L4\n- Lesson Role: Language presentation and practice 2 (he / she)\n\n## Day Focus\n- Build on Week 1 people words by introducing he / she and simple statements and questions about age (He's / She's six).\n\n## Displayed Content\n\n### Circle Time\n- Topic: Friends at school — who is in our class? (recycles man / woman / boy / girl / friend from Week 1)\n- Song: If You're Happy (Super Simple Songs, 09. 情绪情感) — one song per week, shared across all 5 days of Week 2\n\n### CLIL Class\n- Lesson Outcome: Learners can make simple statements and ask simple questions using he / she with teacher support.\n- Source: TB p11 | PB p11 | AB p11 | Audio 1.08, 1.09, 1.10, AB 1.06, 1.07\n- New Keywords: He's / She's (six), He / She isn't (six), Is he / she (six)?, Yes, he / she is., No, he / she isn't., How old is he / she?\n- Recycled Keywords: colours, family, numbers, friend, picture\n- Target Sentences: He's / She's (six). | Is he / she (six)? — Yes, he / she is. / No, he / she isn't.\n- Activities and Games:\n  1. Presentation / Input — Listen and point to the people scene; meet Cameron, Matt and friends; model He's / She's with a real classmate.\n  2. Guided Practice — He / She sorting game: hold up a boy/girl flashcard, class calls \"He's!\" / \"She's!\"; then add age (six / seven).\n  3. Game / Application — \"Guess the friend\": teacher describes a hidden classmate (\"She's six. She's happy.\"), learners guess who; swap to learner-led with support.\n\n### Phonics\n- Content: TBD (see Unit Open Items — phonics explicit from Week 3, first appears Day 11 / L7)\n\n### Story\n- Book / Story: Our Class is a Family (Shannon Olsen) — picture-book, one book per week\n- Daily Focus (Day 1): Picture walk — explore the cover and first spreads; predict what \"our class is a family\" might mean; notice the teacher and children as a community.\n\n## Source Audit\n- Original New Language: He's/She's (six), He/She isn't (six), Is he/she (six)?, Yes, he/she is., No, he/she isn't., How old is he/she?\n- Original Recycled Language: colours, family, numbers, friend, picture\n- Current Classification Result: New / recycled split carried from source; he/she age statements are the new focus of Week 2.\n\n## Review Issues\n- Needs Extension:\n  - colours (recycled — keep warm)\n  - numbers (recycled — used for age)\n- Source Issues:\n  - None for this day.\n",
